  <section data-figma-id="271:77" class="d-557c503f2">
    <p data-figma-id="275:80" class="d-33bfcec94">
      HOW I GOT THE JOB
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="275:81" class="d-251e8a094">
      <span class="d-6f49e1dd1">
        I emailed a company I wanted to work with, showed them my portfolio, and sat through{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-fae2382b9">four interviews</span>
      <span class="d-6f49e1dd1">
        {' '}
        to find out whether I was good enough. Then they said yes, handed me a brief, and gave me a
        month.
      </span>
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="275:82" class="d-2cc83b954">
      No team. No design system. One manager, and a document telling me what they wanted.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:78" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="275:83" class="d-5a9067f48">
      01 — THE PROBLEM
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="275:84" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      Cyclomedia drives 100 cars around the world photographing streets. Their competitors don't
      send a memo.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="275:85" class="d-839d7df68">
      Cyclomedia maps roads and infrastructure — over a million kilometres a year, sold to city
      councils, utilities and insurers in more than 30 countries. Big company, offices in the
      Netherlands and the US.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="275:86" class="d-839d7df68">
      Meanwhile their competitors are busy too. Someone launches an AI feature. Someone quietly
      drops their price. Someone wins a contract in a city Cyclomedia was hoping to win. All of this
      is public. All of it is findable. And nobody has three hours a day to go find it.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="275:87" class="d-839d7df68">
      My brief asked for an automated tool that checks competitors and sends a short weekly update
      to Microsoft Teams. It also contained one sentence in capital letters that I ended up taping
      to my brain:
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="274:73" class="d-df1963cc2">
      <div data-figma-id="274:74" class="d-91f364f31"></div>
      <h2 data-figma-id="274:75" class="d-528f1570f">
        'Do NOT start with UI components. Start with system thinking.'
      </h2>
      <div data-figma-id="274:76" class="d-91f364f31"></div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="275:92" class="d-839d7df68">
      Fair enough. So I did. And then I immediately made a completely different mistake.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:79" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="275:93" class="d-5a9067f48">
      02 — VERSION 1
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="275:94" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      I built a beautiful homepage for a product nobody needs to be sold.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="275:95" class="d-839d7df68">
      My first version opened with a proper marketing page. Big headline. Hero image. A menu with
      Product, How It Works, Company. A Log In button in the corner. It looked like a real startup.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="275:96" class="d-d55c5b1d5">
      <div data-figma-id="276:106" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:107" class="d-c9c8a8442">
          <img
            alt="v1 landing — version1 landing page with amarketing headline hero image and aproduct how it works company menu"
            src="/images/cyclointel/v1-landing-png-version1-landing-page-with-amarketing-headline-hero-image-and-aproduct-how--8bfe1.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-051f5c546">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            V1 — THE LANDING PAGE
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            'Stay ahead of your competitors with automated insights.' Nice line. Written for a
            customer who does not exist.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="275:101" class="d-839d7df68">
      Then I thought about who would actually type this URL. Cyclomedia employees. People who
      already know what the tool is, why it exists, and who asked for it. I had built a shop window
      for a room that only staff can enter.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="275:102" class="d-839d7df68">
      So I deleted it and folded it into the login screen instead. One page: you arrive, you log in,
      you're in.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="275:103" class="d-d55c5b1d5">
