  <section data-figma-id="271:81" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="276:151" class="d-5a9067f48">
      04 — THE DISAGREEMENT
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:152" class="d-06277231a">
      <p class="d-ef8f532c0">My manager wanted a like button.</p>
      <p class="d-7a9a2bbfb">I said no.</p>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:153" class="d-839d7df68">
      Her idea: put thumbs up, thumbs down and comments on every update. Popular items float to the
      top, and the reactions help the system learn what's important.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:154" class="d-839d7df68">
      It's a reasonable idea. I still thought it was wrong, for three reasons.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:155" class="d-839d7df68">
      First, the whole product exists because the AI already does the filtering, carefully, with a
      score. If I then let votes reorder the feed, I've built a popularity contest on top of a
      judgement I just spent a month designing.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:156" class="d-839d7df68">
      Second, votes and comments are work — and my brief specifically said the system must not
      depend on people maintaining it.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:157" class="d-839d7df68">
      Third, a loud team can out-vote a quiet one. The signal that matters enormously to two people
      in Sales gets buried by the one everyone finds interesting.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:158" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">So I proposed three smaller things instead: mark it </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">Relevant</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> (a quiet nod to colleagues, no ranking power), </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">bookmark</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> it for yourself, and </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">share</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> it with someone who needs it.</span>
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:159" class="d-8905316eb">
      <div data-figma-id="276:160" class="d-2e94db1ba">
        <div data-figma-id="276:161" class="d-1a5c4dc24">
          <img
            alt="relevant — the final signal card actions relevant bookmark and share"
            src="/images/cyclointel/relevant-png-the-final-signal-card-actions-relevant-bookmark-and-share-47528.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-327af87d5">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            FINAL
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            Three actions. None of them changes the order of the feed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:82" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="276:165" class="d-5a9067f48">
      05 — WHAT I BUILT
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:166" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      The final version starts with a subtraction.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:167" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        Seven signals came in. Four are worth your time. That's the first sentence of the product,
        before any data appears, because the filtering{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-75cfe8f5f">is</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> the feature.</span>
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:168" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        And every update answers two questions, not one. What happened — and then{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">So what</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        : what this means for Cyclomedia and what to do about it. &quot;Ecopia dropped their price
        18%&quot; is news. &quot;Expect pressure on the Texas and Florida renewals where they're
        shortlisted — lead with the 5-year cost calculator&quot; is something you can use before
        lunch.
      </span>
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:169" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:170" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:171" class="d-345065c4e">
          <img
            alt="insights — final insights feed headed good morning heres what moved today with four stat tiles and filters"
            src="/images/cyclointel/insights-png-final-insights-feed-headed-good-morning-heres-what-moved-today-with-four-stat-fc48f.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-23c40cee0">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            INSIGHTS
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            '7 signals scanned. 4 updates worth your time.' One column, most important first, and it
            ends — no infinite scroll.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div data-figma-id="276:175" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:176" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:177" class="d-345065c4e">
          <img
            alt="signals — two signal cards with why it matters blocks confidence bars and source links"
            src="/images/cyclointel/signals-png-two-signal-cards-with-why-it-matters-blocks-confidence-bars-and-source-links-d1455.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-23c40cee0">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            A SIGNAL
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            Who, what type, which layer of the market, how big, the fact, the 'so what', how sure
            the AI is, and the source. Enough to act or to ignore without clicking anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:83" class="d-831aa85b1">
    <p data-figma-id="276:181" class="d-5a9067f48">
      06 — THE PART I'M PROUDEST OF
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:182" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      The system says out loud when it isn't sure.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:183" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        My earlier version had one number in the header: 98% AI confidence, forever. It comforts
        nobody. It tells you nothing about{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-75cfe8f5f">this</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> update — the one you're about to forward to your boss.</span>
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:184" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        So confidence moved onto every individual signal. 92%. 84%. 88%. And once it lives there,
        something new becomes possible: the system can notice when something is{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">very important and not very certain</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> — and refuse to publish it.</span>
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="274:73" class="d-df1963cc2">
      <div data-figma-id="274:74" class="d-91f364f31"></div>
      <h2 data-figma-id="274:75" class="d-528f1570f">
        'Ecopia reportedly in talks to acquire StreetScan for $28M — unconfirmed.' Confidence: 41%.
        Two blog posts, no press release. A human decides this one.
      </h2>
      <div data-figma-id="274:76" class="d-91f364f31"></div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:189" class="d-839d7df68">
      That's the Review Queue. Rumours that would matter enormously if true don't go out
      automatically. Someone approves, rejects, or edits them first.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:190" class="d-839d7df68">
      And it keeps the receipts: 14 reviewed this month, 11 approved, 3 rejected — including a HERE
      acquisition rumour that turned out to be nonsense. Those three rejections are the reason to
      trust the other eleven.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:191" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:192" class="d-ce8671938">
        <div data-figma-id="276:193" class="d-2c7332809">
          <img
            alt="review queue — review queue with two pending signals flagged high impact but low confidence with approve reject and edit buttons"
            src="/images/cyclointel/review-queue-png-review-queue-with-two-pending-signals-flagged-high-impact-but-low-confide-ac6b9.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-5902df3f7">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            REVIEW QUEUE
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            It explains itself: 'source reliability is low — 2 blog posts, no press release.'
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="276:197" class="d-ce8671938">
        <div data-figma-id="276:198" class="d-2c7332809">
          <img
            alt="review log — recently reviewed list and monthly review statistics"
