          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-5902df3f7">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            THE RECEIPTS
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            A visible rejection rate, including the one it got wrong.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:84" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="276:202" class="d-5a9067f48">
      07 — WHO'S READING
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:203" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      A price cut is an emergency for Sales and background noise for Engineering.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:204" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        This was the idea I liked most and it's embarrassingly simple:{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">the system already knows who just logged in.</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e"> So it should know what that person cares about.</span>
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:205" class="d-839d7df68">
      Sales weights pricing changes and tender results high, product launches low. Product does the
      exact opposite. Same signal, one pipeline, different priority depending on who it reaches —
      and it lands in a different Teams channel for each team. Leadership gets summary only, because
      seniority means less detail, not more.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:206" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:207" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:208" class="d-345065c4e">
          <img
            alt="team switch — account menu showing aviewing as list with sales product engineering strategy and leadership"
            src="/images/cyclointel/team-switch-png-account-menu-showing-aviewing-as-list-with-sales-product-engineering-strat-7d08b.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-23c40cee0">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            VIEWING AS
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            Five audiences, one pipeline. In the prototype you can switch teams to see the weighting
            actually change.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:85" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="276:212" class="d-5a9067f48">
      08 — USING WHAT THEY ALREADY HAD
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:213" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      Cyclomedia had already written the answers down.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:214" class="d-839d7df68">
      They keep battle cards — documents on each competitor with their pricing, their weaknesses,
      and prepared counter-arguments. Good documents. Also frozen in time from the day they were
      saved.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:215" class="d-839d7df68">
      My brief said use them, so I did — as the system's background knowledge. That's what turns a
      summary into an argument. Here's the actual trace:
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="278:153" class="d-7fbeedfcc">
      <div data-figma-id="278:154" class="d-de3a71976">
        <p data-figma-id="278:155" class="d-fc391af82">
          WHAT THE BATTLE CARD ALREADY SAID
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="278:156" class="d-02f1cd9e1">
          <span class="d-47084631e">iNovitas, Switzerland. Surveys at </span>
          <span class="d-cb8cca44c">€60–100/km</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">
            . Customers get limited usage rights — the data stays theirs. Around{' '}
          </span>
          <span class="d-cb8cca44c">15 AI object classes, versus our 32</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">. No historical time series.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="278:157" class="d-fbf17a7cb">
        <p data-figma-id="278:158" class="d-fc391af82">
          WHAT THE SYSTEM WROTE
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="278:159" class="d-02f1cd9e1">
          <span class="d-47084631e">&quot;iNovitas cuts survey pricing to </span>
          <span class="d-cb8cca44c">€65/km</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e"> for mid-size German municipalities.&quot; </span>
          <span class="d-75cfe8f5f">So what:</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e"> lead with our historical time series, </span>
          <span class="d-cb8cca44c">32 AI classes against their ~15</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">
            , and full data ownership. Their model still locks customers in.
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:218" class="d-839d7df68">
      Nothing there is invented. It's Cyclomedia's own positioning, pulled out at the moment it
      became useful.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:219" class="d-839d7df68">
      And it works the other way too. If the card says a competitor is weak at AI and they then ship
      three AI features, that contradiction is itself worth knowing. So the profile is built as a
      living page: the stable knowledge in tabs — including Battle Notes — and the live signals
      running down the side.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:220" class="d-d55c5b1d5">
      <div data-figma-id="276:221" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:222" class="d-345065c4e">
          <img
            alt="profile — here technologies profile with tabs for overview business model coverage technical swot and battle notes athreat meter and recent signals"
            src="/images/cyclointel/profile-png-here-technologies-profile-with-tabs-for-overview-business-model-coverage-techn-dad59.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-23c40cee0">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            COMPETITOR PROFILE
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            The battle card's own sections became the tabs. Nobody has to learn a new document.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:226" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        One more thing about competitors: in this industry the dangerous one usually isn't the
        obvious one. A camera manufacturer is harmless right up until the day it adds AI analysis
        and lands in your business. So competitors are organised by{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">where they sit in the value chain</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        {' '}
        — hardware, positioning, data, software — and one company can sit in several at once.
      </span>
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:227" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:228" class="d-ce8671938">
        <div data-figma-id="276:229" class="d-2c7332809">
          <img
            alt="competitors — final competitors page with tabs for hardware positioning data and insights software"
            src="/images/cyclointel/competitors-png-final-competitors-page-with-tabs-for-hardware-positioning-data-and-insight-7779b.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-5902df3f7">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            COMPETITORS
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            The tabs are the market's structure, not a filing system I invented.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="276:233" class="d-ce8671938">
        <div data-figma-id="276:234" class="d-2c7332809">
          <img
            alt="add competitor — add competitor dialog with multi select value chain layer checkboxes"
            src="/images/cyclointel/add-competitor-png-add-competitor-dialog-with-multi-select-value-chain-layer-checkboxes-899d0.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
