  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:86" class="d-4bfe5e2ed">
    <p data-figma-id="276:238" class="d-5a9067f48">
      09 — BORING BUT IMPORTANT
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:239" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      A robot that breaks silently is worse than no robot.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:240" class="d-839d7df68">
      Here's the nightmare: a scraper stops working, the feed gets thinner, and a quiet week looks
      exactly like a broken system. Everyone keeps trusting it while it watches nothing.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:241" class="d-839d7df68">
      So the machinery is visible where it needs to be. Ten agents, each with a success rate. One is
      rate-limited. One has been offline for two days and says so in red, with a restart button. I
      left the broken one in the prototype on purpose — a screen full of green ticks would prove
      less.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:242" class="d-f6aa38a40">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        Same for money. Every service is listed with what it costs: about{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">$169 a month</span>
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        , all in. If someone has to justify this tool to a finance team, that's a design problem
        too.
      </span>
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:243" class="d-381125eff">
