  <section data-figma-id="271:87" class="d-831aa85b1">
    <p data-figma-id="276:259" class="d-5a9067f48">
      10 — WHERE IT ENDS UP
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:260" class="d-b5d2c0db8">
      The real product isn't the dashboard. It's a message on Friday morning.
    </h2>
    <p data-figma-id="276:261" class="d-839d7df68">
      Building a dashboard is the easy answer, and it sneakily hands the work back to the user:
      here's everything, go find what matters. The brief wanted the opposite — a short weekly update
      in Microsoft Teams, three to five items, readable in under five minutes.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:262" class="d-839d7df68">
      So the digest is the product and the dashboard is where you go when you want more.
      Intelligence should turn up in the app people already have open, not in one more thing to
      remember to check.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:263" class="d-f46b3bfac">
      <div data-figma-id="276:264" class="d-53cd05d2f">
        <div data-figma-id="276:265" class="d-345065c4e">
          <img
            alt="digest — teams digest showing the top three insights of the week with ateam switcher"
            src="/images/cyclointel/digest-png-teams-digest-showing-the-top-three-insights-of-the-week-with-ateam-switcher-a58d4.webp"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="d-b9e4ea293"
          />
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="272:73" class="d-23c40cee0">
          <p data-figma-id="272:75" class="d-a2feafe27">
            WEEKLY DIGEST
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="272:76" class="d-414ecf017">
            Top three. Not top twenty. 'Only what matters' is the promise the whole system exists to
            keep.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section data-figma-id="271:88" class="d-c4f75b1e9">
    <p data-figma-id="276:269" class="d-33bfcec94">
      11 — HONESTLY
    </p>
    <h2 data-figma-id="276:270" class="d-305dd0112">
      What's real, and what I'd check next.
    </h2>
    <div data-figma-id="276:271" class="d-4809a6258">
      <p data-figma-id="278:160" class="d-fc2877dfd">
        BEFORE ANYONE ASKS
      </p>
      <div data-figma-id="278:161" class="d-6c8432e3b">
        <p data-figma-id="278:162" class="d-42f869311">
          REAL
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="278:163" class="d-8ab103f89">
          The research, the way competitors are modelled, the signal categories, the scoring and
          routing logic, the trust rules, and a working clickable prototype.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="278:164" class="d-6c8432e3b">
        <p data-figma-id="278:165" class="d-42f869311">
          MADE UP
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="278:166" class="d-8ab103f89">
          Every competitor update and number inside the prototype. The pipeline is designed, not
          running.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="278:167" class="d-6c8432e3b">
        <p data-figma-id="278:168" class="d-42f869311">
          UNTESTED
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="278:169" class="d-8ab103f89">
          Whether people trust a score a machine gave them. That's the big one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:272" class="d-2cc83b954">
      If this got built, three questions would decide whether it works — and none of them is
      answered by a prettier screen.
    </p>
    <div data-figma-id="276:273" class="d-3e43daffb">
      <div data-figma-id="279:138" class="d-9f430b34d">
        <p data-figma-id="279:139" class="d-641403c32">
          01
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="279:140" class="d-d63d83ac9">
          <span class="d-b38ffe501">Do people trust the impact score?</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">
            {' '}
            I'd give five colleagues 30 signals to rank by hand, then compare their order to the
            system's. If they disagree wildly, the scoring is wrong and everything downstream is
            theatre.
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="279:141" class="d-9f430b34d">
        <p data-figma-id="279:142" class="d-641403c32">
          02
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="279:143" class="d-d63d83ac9">
          <span class="d-b38ffe501">Is weekly fast enough?</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">
            {' '}
            I'd take ten real competitive decisions and work backwards to when the signal first
            became public. If most had a deadline inside a week, the cadence is wrong.
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="279:144" class="d-975de529c">
        <p data-figma-id="279:145" class="d-641403c32">
          03
        </p>
        <p data-figma-id="279:146" class="d-d63d83ac9">
          <span class="d-b38ffe501">Does anyone actually clear the review queue?</span>
          <span class="d-47084631e">
            {' '}
            If those uncertain signals sit there for days, the safest part of the system quietly
            becomes the most ignored.
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p data-figma-id="276:274" class="d-2cc83b954">
      I presented all of this in the final meeting. They accepted it.
    </p>
    <p data-figma-id="276:275" class="d-251e8a094">
      <span class="d-47084631e">
        What I'd take into the next project is smaller than the project itself:{' '}
      </span>
      <span class="d-6df3d0592">
        the hardest part wasn't collecting information or designing screens. It was deciding what
        &quot;important&quot; means, and then being willing to throw away 241 things out of 248.
      </span>
    </p>
